\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Post-training with reinforcement learning (RL) is what turns a pretrained autoregressive (AR) language model into a model that reasons \citep{ouyang2022instructgpt,shao2024deepseekmath,deepseek2025r1}. The language model is a policy whose action is the next token: at every position it outputs a categorical distribution over the vocabulary and samples one token from it. A completion is sampled, a reward is obtained from a verifier or a reward model, an advantage is formed against a baseline or a group of samples, and the score-function policy gradient \citep{williams1992reinforce} raises the log-probability of every token of a rewarded completion and lowers it for an unrewarded one, with PPO or GRPO supplying the trust region and the baseline \citep{schulman2017ppo,shao2024deepseekmath}. This works because the action space is made for it: every action is a discrete, low-dimensional, semantically meaningful decision with an explicit probability, and credit attaches to exactly those decisions.

A new family of language models takes no token actions at all. Continuous flow language models such as ELF \citep{hu2026elf} represent a sequence as real-valued token embeddings and generate it by flow matching \citep{lipman2022flow,liu2022rectified}: starting from Gaussian noise, a denoiser transports the whole sequence toward clean embeddings over a number of \emph{flow steps}, and tokens are read out only at the end. Sampling is a chain of Gaussian transitions over an $L\times d$ real-valued state, there is no token distribution to sample from along the way, and the entire chain is differentiable. How to post-train such a model is open. The tempting answer is to port the AR recipe by treating each denoising step as an action with a Gaussian likelihood, as has been done for image flows \citep{black2023ddpo,liu2025flowgrpo,xue2025dancegrpo} and for masked diffusion language models \citep{zhao2025d1,zhu2025llada15}.

We argue that this port is structurally wrong for a continuous flow language model, and then measure it (\S\ref{sec:estimators}). The ``actions'' of the flow model are not decisions; they are the noise coordinates injected at every step, $L\times d$ of them per step and $K$ steps per sample. No single coordinate decides a token, because a token is committed only by the accumulated dynamics of the whole chain. The score-function gradient of a Gaussian step is the injected noise times a Jacobian, so every reward bit is averaged over an astronomically larger set of nuisance directions than in the AR case, where it attaches to a few dozen categorical decisions. This is a property of the estimator, not of the advantage: exact advantages, credit assigned per step and per position, per-position trust regions and critic-selected advantages all leave the same small, noisy signal, while an AR model trained with the same estimator on the same data pulls away from all of them.

The alternative uses what the flow model has and the AR model lacks: differentiability. The pathwise (reparameterised) gradient is absent from AR post-training only because categorical sampling cannot be reparameterised; here it is the native estimator, the one used in continuous control and in reward back-propagation for image diffusion \citep{lillicrap2015ddpg,haarnoja2018sac,prabhudesai2023alignprop,clark2023draft}. Two obstacles remain: the reward exists only on the decoded text at the end of the chain, and back-propagating through all $K$ steps is expensive and a known route to reward hacking. There is also a question of \emph{where} credit should be indexed. In AR post-training every quantity the algorithm manipulates, the action, the advantage, the value, is indexed by the token position alone, because the token position is the RL time step; in a flow model a generation is a grid of states over flow steps and token positions, and existing ports keep a single axis, the flow step, discarding the token axis on which the semantics live. Our answer to both obstacles is a \hl{two-dimensional (2D) critic} (Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism}), a learned value defined over the two axes along which credit lives in a flow model: the \emph{flow-step} axis, i.e.\ \emph{when} along the denoising trajectory a noisy intermediate state is evaluated, and the \emph{token-position} axis, i.e.\ \emph{where} along the sequence the outcome is decided. Given any intermediate state at any flow step, the critic outputs one value per token position and pools them into a value of the state. The policy is updated by back-propagating that pooled value through the denoiser at a single random flow step, which keeps memory independent of the chain length; the critic is trained jointly with the policy, from verifier outcomes, on the policy's own rollouts, under the sampler used at deployment. A scalar critic on the pooled state, as in prior reward back-propagation work, is the special case that pools before it evaluates; the 2D form is what makes joint training stable across tasks and seeds. We call the resulting actor--critic recipe \jpc{} (joint pathwise critic).

Around this core we assemble a complete post-training framework for continuous flow language models: an analysis of which policy-gradient estimator the architecture admits, the \jpc{} actor--critic stage with its stopping rule, and an amortisation stage (\S\ref{sec:loop}) that distils the RL policy's verified samples back into the model by supervised fine-tuning, so that samplers far cheaper than the one used for RL inherit the improvement. We evaluate on four reasoning tasks with program verifiers (Countdown, Sudoku, Knights-and-Knaves, and a Game24 split that tests generalisation to unseen number sets), with multiple seeds, against ported score-function baselines and against an AR model trained with the standard recipe on the same data.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
(i) A structural argument, with matching measurements, for why score-function RL is capped on continuous flow language models and why pathwise gradients are their native estimator (\S\ref{sec:estimators}).
(ii) The \hl{2D critic}, a value over flow steps and token positions that turns the pathwise gradient into a practical actor--critic method, \jpc{}, whose ingredients (per-position values pooled to a state value, joint training, the deployment sampler, one random step per update, early stopping on a held-out set) we ablate individually (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:ablations}).
(iii) To our knowledge, the first demonstration that RL post-training reliably improves a continuous flow language model: across four tasks and multiple seeds, \jpc{} is the only recipe that improves every task at the deployment sampler, with its largest gains where supervised fine-tuning cannot help (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).
(iv) An amortisation stage that carries the RL gains to cheap samplers, with controls that attribute the gains to the RL policy, and an account of where the framework does not help (\S\ref{sec:loop}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}).
